Appendix B — Directed Evolution Platforms: Technical Specifications and Performance Data

B.1 eVOLVER Continuous‑Culture Array

Principle: A modular, open‑source platform for multiplexed, automated continuous culture of microorganisms. Each vessel is fitted with an optical density (OD) sensor, a magnetic stirrer, and fluidic connections for media input and culture removal. A central microcontroller runs a PID feedback loop: when OD exceeds a user‑defined threshold, a dilution event is triggered, replacing a fraction of the culture with fresh medium, thereby maintaining the population in exponential growth within a defined density window [9].

Key performance metrics from the original publication:

· 16 parallel vessels per module, scalable to hundreds.
· OD measurement precision ±0.02 at 600 nm.
· Dilution timing resolution <1 s.
· Longest continuous run reported: 1,500 hours (>62 days) without contamination in S. cerevisiae evolution experiments.
· Selection pressure programmable as a function of any measured parameter (pH, dissolved oxygen, fluorescence from reporter genes).

Lunar Adaptation: The breeder will run eVOLVER‑type arrays with lunar regolith simulant as a medium component, under reduced‑pressure (0.7 atm) and elevated CO₂ (up to 5000 ppm) to mimic habitat conditions. Mutagenesis can be combined with the ACE system (see B.2).

---

B.2 ACE (Automated Continuous Evolution)

Origin: Liu et al., 2020, bioRxiv preprint (now published in Nature Methods 2021).
Mechanism: A chemostat housing an orthogonal DNA polymerase (e.g., an error‑prone variant of E. coli Pol I) that continuously introduces mutations into a target gene without arresting culture growth. The dilution rate is controlled by a PID loop that links stringency to the activity of the target gene product (e.g., antibiotic resistance, metabolic output). Thus, populations autonomously navigate complex fitness landscapes toward improved function [10].

Proof‑of‑Concept Data:

· 5‑fold increase in T7 RNA polymerase activity in 8 days.
· Simultaneous evolution of multiple loci (promoter, RBS, coding sequence) without researcher intervention.
· Sequencing of endpoint populations revealed >100 distinct haplotypes, demonstrating that the system explores sequence space broadly.

Breeder’s Use: Pairing ACE with an eVOLVER array allows simultaneous, autonomous directed evolution of multiple microbial strains toward desired functions (e.g., accelerated cellulose degradation, enhanced nitrogen fixation) under lunar‑specific selection pressures.

---

B.3 PACE (Phage‑Assisted Continuous Evolution)

Relevance: The principle of coupling growth rate to target function (via M13 phage infection) is the fastest continuous evolution method known. While PACE is currently limited to protein evolution in vitro, its conceptual framework — linking fitness to a measurable output and automating dilution — is the philosophical foundation for all continuous‑culture breeding platforms the lunar breeder will deploy.

