Appendix C — Mathematical Proofs: Early Warning Signals for Critical Transitions

This appendix formalizes the mechanism of critical slowing down (CSD) and derives the statistical indicators used in the Breeder’s Dashboard (Section 7.4). The derivations follow the seminal work of Scheffer et al. (2009, 2012) [11][12] and subsequent deep‑learning implementations [13].

C.1 Bifurcation Model

Consider a one‑dimensional dynamical system near a fold bifurcation:

\frac{dx}{dt} = r - x^2

where x is the state variable (e.g., algal biomass) and r is the control parameter (e.g., nutrient loading). The equilibrium points satisfy x^* = \pm\sqrt{r} for r>0; a bifurcation occurs at r=0. For r slowly approaching zero from above, the dominant eigenvalue (recovery rate) is:

\lambda = \left.\frac{d(-x^2)}{dx}\right|_{x=\sqrt{r}} = -2\sqrt{r}

As r \to 0, \lambda \to 0; the system recovers from perturbations more and more slowly — this is critical slowing down.

C.2 Autocorrelation Increase

A small stochastic perturbation \sigma \xi(t) (white noise) added to the linearized dynamics yields an Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process:

dx = \lambda x\, dt + \sigma\, dW

The autocorrelation function is \rho(\tau) = e^{\lambda |\tau|}. The lag‑1 autocorrelation of a discretely sampled time series (sampling interval \Delta t) is:

AR1 = e^{\lambda \Delta t} \approx 1 + \lambda \Delta t

As \lambda becomes less negative (approach to bifurcation), AR1 increases toward 1. A rising AR1 is therefore an indicator of an approaching transition. Statistical significance is assessed via Kendall’s \tau trend test on a sliding window of AR1 values.

C.3 Variance Increase

The stationary variance of the Ornstein–Uhlenbeck process is:

\text{Var}(x) = \frac{\sigma^2}{2|\lambda|}

As |\lambda| \to 0, the variance diverges. In practice, variance is computed over a rolling window; an upward trend signals loss of resilience.

C.4 Flickering and Skewness

When stochastic forcing is strong enough, the system can “flicker” between the two basins of attraction of the bistable regime (r<0), causing temporary excursions before the actual bifurcation. This appears as:

· Increased skewness of the state distribution.
· Spectral reddening (power concentrates at low frequencies).
· Flickering: abrupt, temporary jumps between distinct states visible in the time series.

The Breeder Dashboard computes these four indicators (AR1, variance, skewness, reddening ratio) simultaneously using a 90‑day rolling window; an alert is raised when at least two indicators show significant positive trends (p<0.01) concurrently.

C.5 Limitations — Shock‑Driven Transitions

The CSD framework assumes a slow, monotonic forcing of the control parameter. It is not applicable to:

· Rate‑induced tipping (forcing faster than the system’s intrinsic relaxation time).
· Noise‑induced transitions (large stochastic kicks that jump the state without a bifurcation).
· External shock events (hardware failure, sudden contamination pulse).

In the Breeder Dashboard, the “Shock Risk” meter is calculated from a separate set of inputs: hardware fault probabilities, crew activity logs, and sudden‑change detection in sensor streams (CUSUM algorithm). It is visually separated from the “Slow Drift” panel (see Section 7.4 of the main document).

