Appendix G — AI Behavioral Auditor Protocol Specification

G.1 Screening Pipeline

Input: A protein sequence S generated or selected by the Breeder’s AI‑assisted design tool.

Step 1 — Sequence homology: BLASTP against the NCBI nr database and a curated lunar‑colony toxin database (derived from all known pathogenicity islands, endotoxins, exotoxins, and ribosome‑inactivating proteins). Threshold: E‑value ≤ 0.001 triggers manual review.

Step 2 — Structure prediction: Run AlphaFold3 (or equivalent) on S to obtain a 3D model.

Step 3 — Structural similarity search: Use Foldseek (fast structural alignment) against the same toxin database, with a TM‑score threshold of 0.5 and an active‑site RMSD < 2.0 Å for any known catalytic triad or binding pocket.

Step 4 — Language model embedding: Extract the internal representation of S from a model fine‑tuned to predict “toxic” vs “non‑toxic” labels on a training set of 50,000 proteins (using ProtBERT or ESM‑2). Compute cosine distance to the centroid of known toxins; flag if distance < 0.2.

Step 5 — Integrated risk score: A weighted logistic regression combining the normalized scores from steps 1–4, calibrated on a holdout set of 10,000 protein sequences with known toxicity phenotypes. A score > 0.5 triggers Step 6.

Step 6 — In vitro validation: Express the protein in a non‑pathogenic E. coli BL21(DE3) strain under the T7 promoter. Test lysate cytotoxicity on Caco‑2 and A549 human cell lines; an IC50 < 10 µg/mL for either cell line constitutes a positive hit and permanently bars the sequence from release.

G.2 Audit Trail Requirements

Every step of the screening, including all intermediate scores and the final human decision, is logged to an immutable append‑only log (blockchain‑anchored with timestamp). This forms the legal record for the Auditor’s mandate under Section 9.2.

